    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Published hydration review</p>
    <h2 id="BhdPublicationTitle-{{ section.id }}"><em>Revisiting the Need for Innovative Oral Hydration Technologies</em></h2>
    <p><strong>Authors:</strong> Genevieve Vine, Simone Persaud, Austin Swiniarski, Eva Rosaasen-Uwiera and Spencer D. Proctor.</p>
    <p><strong>Journal:</strong> <em>Frontiers in Nutrition</em>, Sport and Exercise Nutrition, Volume 13 (2026).</p>
    <p><strong>Published:</strong> September 23, 2026. <strong>DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.3389/fnut.2026.1851906" target="_blank" rel="noopener">10.3389/fnut.2026.1851906</a>.</p>
    <p>The review examines recent literature on oral rehydration solution formulation and application, including exercise rehydration, chronic-disease contexts, sodium-glucose transport and opportunities for further oral hydration technology development.</p>
    <p>This is a review of the broader evidence base. It is not a clinical efficacy trial of the finished Best Hydrate product, and the paper should not be read as proof of product-specific clinical outcomes.</p>
    <div class="bhd-page__brand-actions">
      <a class="bhd-page__button" href="https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/nutrition/articles/10.3389/fnut.2026.1851906/abstract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the Frontiers article →</a>
      <a class="bhd-page__button bhd-page__button--secondary" href="/blogs/news/just-published-innovative-oral-hydration-technologies">Publication announcement →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Why this matters</span>
    <h3>Oral hydration technology is still an active research field.</h3>
    <p>The paper connects established oral rehydration physiology with newer formulation and delivery questions, and identifies areas where additional evidence and technology development may be useful.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

